[
  {
    "text": "Reactive policy benchmarks in embodied AI (e.g., PointGoal navigation, some Habitat tasks) do not require translating long-term memory into plans because optimal policies can be computed from current observations and local information, eliminating the need for explicit stateful memory.",
    "topic": "Reactive benchmark design"
  },
  {
    "text": "Partial observability tasks can be partitioned into memoryless (Markov) and memory-requiring (non-Markov) classes; the challenge of translating long-term memory into embodied plans is only relevant for the latter.",
    "topic": "Memory requirement taxonomy"
  },
  {
    "text": "The claim that translating long-term memory into embodied plans remains a persistent challenge is consistent with the existence of reactive solutions if the original statement refers to systems that aim for general, long-horizon task performance, not all embodied agents.",
    "topic": "Scope of challenge statement"
  },
  {
    "text": "Empirical evidence on overwritten world states shows that without an explicit memory-to-plan component, reactive agents fail in environments where past actions irreversibly alter future state availability, such as object removal or navigation with dead ends.",
    "topic": "Failure modes under overwritten states"
  }
]
